// Klinkhåmer Special: Hans van Klinken's parachute emerger, hanging in the film on a curved hook.

id klinkhamer-special
name "Klinkhåmer Special"
aliases "Klinkhammer" "Klinki" "LT Caddis"
type emerger
imitates caddis mayfly
difficulty intermediate
species trout grayling
season may june july august september
origin originator="Hans van Klinken" year=1984 region="Glomma, Norway"
description "A slim tan abdomen round the curve of the hook, a peacock herl thorax, a white post and a long parachute hackle."
history """
    Hans van Klinken, a Dutch army instructor, first fished it on 27 June 1984 on the Glomma
    in Norway. He called it the LT Caddis, one fly of an LT series he developed in Scandinavia;
    Hans de Groot and Ton Lindhout, his colleagues on a magazine's editorial staff, renamed it
    the Klinkhåmer Special. It grew from Kenneth Boström's Rackelhanen: van Klinken's copies
    sometimes landed wing-flat, so he wound a hackle round the wing, as he had seen in a book
    by Eric Leiser, and a few months later added the herl thorax. He points out that others
    had the same idea: Tomas Olsen in Sweden a year earlier, Roy Richardson in the United
    States in 1986, and Mike Monroe, whose Paratilt was published in 1979.
    """
fishing """
    The abdomen hangs below the surface like a hatching insect while the parachute holds the
    fly up; van Klinken says it suggests all kinds of emerging mayflies and caddis. He
    designed it for grayling, which tend to push high-floating flies aside as they take, and
    finds it hooks them well. It stays upright in riffles and strong currents, and the white
    post shows the takes of fish that sip it down without a ring. Treat the post and hackle
    with floatant but not the body, which has to break through the film.
    """
sources {
    "Fly Fisherman: The Klinkhåmer Special at 40: Still One of the Best (Hans van Klinken)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/40-years-klinkhamer-special/490681"
    "Tom Sutcliffe: Hans van Klinken on his Klinkhåmer Special (Hans van Klinken)" url="https://www.tomsutcliffe.co.za/fly-fishing/fly-tying/item/248-hans-van-klinken-on-his-klinkhamer-special.html"
    "Rackelhanen Flyfishing Magazine: How to use our Klinkhåmer Special correctly (Hans van Klinken, archived)" url="https://web.archive.org/web/20071211113302/http://www.rackelhanen.se/eng/10342.htm"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Klinkhammer_Special_Dry_Fly.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klinkhammer_Special_Dry_Fly.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" note="Side view, replica of the original"
    "https://www.tomsutcliffe.co.za/newsletters/The_Klinkhamer_special_3.jpg" page="https://www.tomsutcliffe.co.za/fly-fishing/fly-tying/item/248-hans-van-klinken-on-his-klinkhamer-special.html" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view, tan body"
}

hook emerger-curved size=12
thread grey size="8/0"

materials {
    post poly-yarn
    // Van Klinken calls for a long hackle ("twice the length of the hook gap or more"); his
    // own photos show fibres about 1.3× the gape from the post, which is used here.
    hackle cock-hackle color=blue-dun fibre=1.3
    abdomen dry-fly-dubbing color=tan label="Light tan poly dubbing"
    thorax peacock-herl strands=3
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.8 note="Spiral the thread over the whole shank and well round the bend first."
    wrap-thread to=0.75
    // Van Klinken: the post at least a shank long, trimmed to taste afterwards.
    tie-in post length=1.0 angle=90 note="Angle-cut so the underbody stays slim; stand it up with posting turns."
    tie-in hackle note="Stem posted up the yarn."
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    dub abdomen to=0.72 note="Slim and tapered, from as far round the bend as possible to the post."
    tie-in thorax note="Three herls by their tips, right behind the post."
    wrap-thread to=0.92
    wind thorax to=0.9 turns=4 note="A robust thorax, in front of and round the base of the post."
    tie-off thorax
    wind hackle around=post turns=6 note="From the top of the post downwards; it is hard to over-hackle."
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish
}
